{
  "$schema": "fbench/v1",
  "name": "Kerosene 30 m pool fire",
  "citation": "Mudan, K.S. (1984). Thermal radiation hazards from hydrocarbon pool fires. Progress in Energy and Combustion Science 10(1), 59-80. Table 4, 'Measured emissive powers and radiation temperatures for various liquid hydrocarbon pool fires'.",
  "dataConfidence": "Medium",
  "description": "Large kerosene pool fires measured with wide-angle radiometers by the Japan Institute for Safety Engineering, tabulated by Mudan (1984) Table 4 as 30-80 m pools with average emissive powers of 10-25 kW/m2 and a radiation temperature of 1600 K. Mudan notes the larger fires radiate less than the smaller ones - the smoke obscuration this model's soot blend exists to represent. This is the first benchmark to exercise that branch at all. In this size range the soot-blend cap governs the prediction: the energy balance chi*Q/(pi*D*L) comes out well above the blend, so the min() picks the blend and the burn rate and radiative fraction barely enter. That makes this bench a direct test of the Mudan correlation rather than of the inputs below it, which is why the assumed burn rate is acceptable here.",
